WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.460
The T cell receptor spots an infected cell.

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.620
Yet its inner tail is tiny and holds no enzyme.

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:14.160
Not even a kinase, an enzyme that adds phosphate groups.

4
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.440
How does it fire the T cell without firing by accident?

5
00:00:19.583 --> 00:00:21.483
The cell splits the job in two.

6
00:00:22.743 --> 00:00:27.963
The alpha and beta chains recognise a short peptide held in an MHC groove.

7
00:00:29.343 --> 00:00:32.703
Beside them sit partner chains called CD3 and zeta.

8
00:00:33.963 --> 00:00:38.763
Their long inner tails carry paired tyrosines at a fixed spacing, called ITAMs.

9
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:41.147
So where is the kinase?

10
00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:46.787
The tyrosine kinase Lck is already anchored in the T cell membrane.

11
00:00:48.007 --> 00:00:52.127
A share of it rides on a co-receptor, CD4 or CD8.

12
00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:56.027
That co-receptor grips the same MHC protein.

13
00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.727
Lck is partly active even at rest.

14
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.100
So active Lck sits right beside those paired tyrosines in a resting cell.

15
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.840
Yet a resting T cell stays quiet, and its tails carry no lasting phosphate.

16
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.360
What holds the switch off?

17
00:01:20.283 --> 00:01:26.463
A phosphatase called CD45 keeps erasing them, because a phosphatase strips phosphate

18
00:01:26.463 --> 00:01:29.723
groups off. Its outer part is tall.

19
00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:34.683
So when the two cells press close, it no longer fits in the narrow gap.

20
00:01:35.963 --> 00:01:38.923
In one leading model that is what lets phosphates stay.

21
00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:42.037
Now watch what reads those marks.

22
00:01:43.497 --> 00:01:48.637
ZAP-70 is a second kinase with two SH2 claws, spaced to fit one pair.

23
00:01:49.937 --> 00:01:53.997
One phosphate holds a single claw weakly, so ZAP-70 slips off.

24
00:01:55.217 --> 00:01:57.377
That keeps a stray mark from firing the cell.

25
00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:01.617
With both tyrosines tagged, both claws clamp tight.

26
00:02:03.097 --> 00:02:06.297
Docking opens ZAP-70, and Lck switches it on.

27
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.120
Here is the first core rule.

28
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:14.280
Two phosphates on partner ITAMs switch on the antigen receptor.

29
00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.400
Once active, ZAP-70 phosphorylates LAT, a membrane scaffold.

30
00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.360
Its fresh phosphotyrosines become docking bays that split the signal three ways.

31
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:30.773
Within seconds, three branches fan out from LAT.

32
00:02:32.313 --> 00:02:35.973
One raises calcium to send NFAT into the nucleus.

33
00:02:37.273 --> 00:02:41.853
A second runs through DAG and protein kinase C to NF-κB.

34
00:02:43.113 --> 00:02:46.893
A third loads Ras to drive ERK and build AP-1.

35
00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:52.977
All three gene-switching proteins meet on target genes such as interleukin two,

36
00:02:53.477 --> 00:02:55.117
driving the T cell to divide.

37
00:02:56.437 --> 00:02:58.957
The receptor never needed its own enzyme.

38
00:03:00.537 --> 00:03:04.837
Partner tails carry the switch, and demanding two phosphates keeps noise from

39
00:03:04.837 --> 00:03:05.517
pulling the trigger.
